--- a/docs/user/ipc/media-and-ui.mdx
+++ b/docs/user/ipc/media-and-ui.mdx
@@ -125,7 +125,9 @@ Requires compositor support for `wlr-screencopy-unstable-v1`. Uses the global `[
 
 In picker mode, choose a connector from the top bar on any screen, or click the desktop to capture the monitor under the cursor. Errors list available connector names when the selector is unknown.
 
-`annotate <path>` edits a file instead of the screen. The image is decoded with the shell's own loader (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, JPEG XL, ICO, SVG, and the other formats used for wallpapers and icons) and opens centered at its native pixel size on the focused output, scaled down when it does not fit. EXIF orientation is applied, so camera and phone photos open upright. Relative paths resolve against the directory the command ran in, and `~` expands. The pointer button stays disabled because no cursor was captured, and **Freeze** is not offered. **Copy** and **Save** deliver the annotated PNG, and **Done** applies the `[shell.screenshot]` output policy. Saving never overwrites the source: it writes a new PNG into the screenshot folder using the configured filename pattern.
+`screenshot-region`, `screenshot-fullscreen`, `screenshot-annotate`, and `annotate` accept `--path <file>` to override the configured output path (for `annotate`, the destination of a saved annotation). An absolute path is used as-is; a relative path resolves against the screenshot output directory; `~` expands to the home directory. `--path` overrides the configured filename pattern and names the exact file (no extension is appended). `screenshot-fullscreen all` and cross-monitor regions still produce a single stitched image, written to that path. Options are separate tokens (`--path /tmp/shot.png`); the `--path=<file>` form is not supported. A missing `--path` value and any unrecognised option (for example `-x` or `--foo`) are rejected with an error. Because the IPC argument is split on whitespace, the `--path` value cannot contain spaces. `--path` only takes effect when `save_to_file` or a `pipe_command` is configured; otherwise the argument is ignored and no file is written.
+
+`annotate <path>` edits a file instead of the screen. The image is decoded with the shell's own loader (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, JPEG XL, ICO, SVG, and the other formats used for wallpapers and icons) and opens centered at its native pixel size on the focused output, scaled down when it does not fit. EXIF orientation is applied, so camera and phone photos open upright. Relative paths resolve against the directory the command ran in, and `~` expands. The pointer button stays disabled because no cursor was captured, and **Freeze** is not offered. **Copy** and **Save** deliver the annotated PNG, and **Done** applies the `[shell.screenshot]` output policy. Saving never overwrites the source: it writes a new PNG into the screenshot folder using the configured filename pattern. Passing `--path` overrides this and names the saved file directly, so a path equal to the source overwrites it.
 
 Annotation tool keys (no modifier): **`m`** move, **`b`** brush, **`h`** highlighter, **`l`** line, **`a`** arrow, **`s`** rectangle, **`o`** circle, **`t`** text, **`n`** numbering, **`u`** blur, **`e`** eraser, **`c`** crop, **`f`** freeze (live overlay), **`p`** toggle the captured pointer, **`[`** and **`]`** change the current tool's size. **`Ctrl+Z`** undoes, **`Ctrl+Shift+Z`** and **`Ctrl+Y`** redo, **`[keybinds].copy`** copies, **`[keybinds].save`** saves.
 
diff --git a/src/capture/screenshot_service.cpp b/src/capture/screenshot_service.cpp
